      "math_academy_id": "q-8718",
      "knowledge_point_id": "ebf8592e-1a43-5d65-af3c-9becd79ae8b9",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Tangent Under a Modified Domain",
      "problem": "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\sqrt{3}$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}=\\pi +{x}_{1}=\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3}=-\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}.\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
