      "knowledge_point": "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Tangent Under a Modified Domain",
      "problem": "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions, in radians, of the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-2$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi ,$ what is the value to $3$ decimal places of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-2)=-1.107\\,148...≈-1.107\\,149\n$$\n\nSince $-1.107\\,149$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-1.107\\,149.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\pi +{x}_{1}=\\pi -1.107\\,148=2.034\\,444.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-1.107\\,149,2.034\\,444.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈-1.107\\,149\\cdot 2.034\\,444≈-2.252\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
