      "math_academy_id": "q-72833",
      "knowledge_point_id": "a71da663-e7a4-5781-973c-5dc7e4deb83f",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Points of Intersection of a Horizontal Line and a Tangent Curve",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981396/assets/aae559883c97bbd19321d9baeb26fe15e47cced5b072e12100c42aa579c9d02d.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and the line $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.$ Find, in radians, the $x$-coordinate of the point $A.$",
      "worked_solution": "The $y$-values of both functions are the same at the intersection point. Equating the two functions gives:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n$$\n\n Now, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies inside the required domain, and there is only one point of intersection, we conclude that the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point $A$ is $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$",
